## Introduction
Coccidioidomycosis is a systemic fungal infection endemic in Southwestern US, Mexico, Central and South America. Although the prevalence of the infection is highest in Southwestern US, and Northern Mexico State, the endemic foci are known in Central and South America [1]. Coccidioidomycosis is caused by the two species of Coccidioides. This distinction was based on the genetic analysis, phenotypic characteristics, and epidemiological features—C. immitis from California, and $C.$ posadasii from outside of California [2]. These two species are also included in the Federal Select Agents lists, requiring strict regulation for laboratory testing [3]. Ongoing studies in Arizona have documented increasing number of cases in humans and animals [4].
Recently, there has been an upsurge of cases of coccidioidomycosis in New York State, a nonendemic area, most likely due to increased travel to and from the Southwest [5, 6]. We have typed many of these isolates as C. posadasii, using molecular markers [5]. Numerous other recent publications have reported coccidioidomycosis from non-endemic areas [7, 8]. Dissemination of Coccidioides infections can present in unusual forms, and these diseases require antifungals with surgical treatment [9]. With the increased incidence of coccidioidomycosis cases in non-endemic areas, diagnostic laboratories often receive requests for antifungal susceptibility testing in cases when patients have not responded to commonly prescribed antifungal drugs [10–12]. Amphotericin B (AMB), itraconazole (ITC), ketoconazole (KTC), and fluconazole (FLC) have been the drugs available for the treatment of coccidioidomycosis, but their use has often been associated with treatment failure, relapses, and antifungal toxicity [11, 12]. More recently, newer antifungal agents, such as voriconazole (VRC), caspofungin (CSP), and posaconazole (PSC) have been used to treat many cases of coccidioidomycosis, and have been studied in experimental animal models, and in in vitro susceptibility testing [13–17]. The literature on antifungal susceptibility testing of these two species is rather limited. Therefore, we compared the susceptibility profiles of C. posadasii and $C.$ . immitis using the CLSI (NCCLS) M38-A broth microdilution protocol [18]. We also compared this reference method to a commercial kit for susceptibility testing, to examine whether the choice of method has a bearing on testing outcome.
## Materials and methods
Strains
Eight Coccidioides immitis isolates were provided by Dr. Edward Desmond, Mycobacteriology and Mycology Section, Microbial Disease Laboratory, California Department of Health Services, Richmond, CA. Ten Coccidioides posadasii isolates were typed using molecular markers as previously described [1, 4]. Additionally, 27 Coccidioides spp. isolates were received for diagnostic workup at the Mycology Laboratory, Wadsworth Center; they were identified by microscopic morphology and Gen Probe1 test. Detailed molecular analyses of these isolates are pending to distinguish between $C$ . immitis and C. posadasii. Before antifungal assays, the cultures were passaged twice on potato glucose agar slants at $35^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to ensure viability.
Antifungal agents
AMB (Sigma Biochemical Company St. Louis, MO), FLC and VRC (Pfizer Inc. New York, NY), ITC and KTC (Research Diagnostics Inc. Flanders, NJ), CSP (Merck Research Laboratories, Rahway, NJ), and PSC (Schering-Plough, Kenilworth, NJ) were obtained from the respective manufacturers. Stock solutions of AMB, ITC, KTC, VRC, and PSC were prepared in dimethyl sulfoxide (Amresco, Solon, OH), and FLC and CSP were prepared in water, at concentrations of $6{,}400~\upmu\mathrm{g/ml}$ , and were stored at $-70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
## CLSI/NCCLS broth microdilution test
The broth microdilution test was performed in accordance with the M38-A (CLSI) protocol [18]. Serial two-fold dilutions of all antifungals $_{(0.03-}$ $16~\mathrm{\upmug/ml})$ except FLC $\left(0.06{-}64\ \mathrm{\textrm{\textmug/ml}}\right)$ were prepared in microtiter plates. The microtiter plates were prepared in bulk, stored at $-70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , and thawed as required. Inoculum was prepared by flooding the 5–7 day old cultures on potato glucose agar slants with $0.85\%$ sterile saline. The conidia and hyphal fragments were harvested by probing with the tip of a Pasteur pipette, and the resultant mixture was adjusted to 0.5 MacFarland standards. The adjusted inoculum was further diluted 1:50 in RPMI 1640, and a $100~\upmu\mathrm{l}$ aliquot was dispensed into microtiter wells. The microtiter plates were incubated at $35^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The MIC was defined as the lowest drug concentration that produced $100\%$ inhibition for AMB and ITC, or $50\%$ inhibition for FLC, KTC, VRC, PSC, and CSP, when compared to growth control. The testing was performed in a BioSafety Level 3 laboratory and was repeated once.
## MFC determination
After determination of MICs, $10~\upmu\mathrm{l}$ from each well were transferred to the corresponding well of a microtiter plate containing $100~\upmu\mathrm{l}$ of fresh RPMI 1640, and the plates were further incubated for $48\mathrm{~h~}$ . The MFC was determined as the lowest-drug concentration that produced no growth.
## Sensititre YeastOne1 (Trek Diagnostic Systems)
The procedure used followed the manufacturer’s protocol. Briefly, inoculum was prepared from $5-$ 7 day old cultures on potato glucose agar slants. The mixture of conidia and hyphal fragments was adjusted to 0.5 MacFarland standards, and $20~\upmu\mathrm{l}$ were inoculated in Sensititre YeastOne1 inoculum broth; $100~\upmu\mathrm{l}$ of this mix were transferred onto each well and covered with an adhesive seal, and the plates were then incubated for $^{48-72\mathrm{~h~}}$ at $35^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The MIC was recorded as the lowest concentration of drug that produced the first change in well coloration.
## Results and discussion
The results indicated relatively superior in vitro activities of the newer antifungals, PSC and VRC, against Coccidioides species. The distribution of MICs of various antifungals against 45 strains of Coccidioides species is summarized in Table 1. These MICs were in the susceptible range for AMB, FLC, ITC, KTC, and VRC, as per breakpoints published for pathogenic yeasts [19]. Among the three newer antifungals, VRC and PSC had the lowest geometric mean MIC values. FLC had highest geometric mean MIC values against Coccidioides isolates after $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ . A comparison of geometric mean MICs for $C.$ . immitis and C. posadasii is presented in Table 2. There was no noticeable difference between the geometric mean MICs of newer and older antifungals. This is the first report in which the antifungal susceptibility profile for the two species of Coccidioides have been directly compared.
In review of previous susceptibility studies of $c$ . immitis/C. posadasii, the MIC ranges were reported as $0.06{-}2.0~\upmu\mathrm{g/ml}$ for AMB, $0.03\mathrm{-}1.0~\upmu\mathrm{g}/$ ml for ITC, and $2.0{-}64.0~\upmu\mathrm{g/ml}$ for FLC [13, 14, 17]. The patterns of susceptibility observed in the present study are consistent with these published ranges, even though different testing methods and isolates were used. For the more recent drugs, VRC and PSC, MIC ranges of $<0.06-1.0~\mathrm{\upmug/ml}$ reported by Gonzalez et al. [14] were considerably higher than values obtained in the present study. Similarly, Cordeiro et al. [13] reported a CSP MIC range of $16.0{-}32.0\upmu\mathrm{g/}$ ml, again considerably higher than the $0.25{-}8.0~\upmu\mathrm{g}/$ ml obtained in the present study. It is not clear whether these incongruent results are due to the testing of isolates with different susceptibility profiles or to variations caused by methodological differences.
The percent agreement between the MICs obtained by Sensititre YeastOne and CLSI M-38A method is presented in Table 3. AMB geometric mean MICs were higher when obtained by Sensititre YeastOne1 than when obtained by the CLSI method. One possible explanation for the discrepancy is differences in the endpoint reading, as discussed by other investigators [20]. Overall, our results and other published studies find no in vitro resistance in Coccidioides speces to any of the antifungals tested, which raises the possibility that routine testing of these potentially dangerous pathogens may not be necessary. Instead, the clinicians could be advised to consult relevant publications on susceptibility results in order to choose appropriate therapy for coccididodomycosis.
